<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!-- androidx.health.connect:connect-client declares minSdk 26; this app ships
         minSdk 24, so the manifest merger fails :app:processReleaseMainManifest with
         "uses-sdk:minSdkVersion 24 cannot be smaller than version 26 declared in
         library [androidx.health.connect:connect-client]".

         Overriding rather than raising the app's minSdk to 26, which would drop
         Android 7.x (API 24/25) devices — a product decision this build failure
         should not force. The override is safe because nothing here is reachable
         below API 26: HealthConnectModule.isSdkAvailable() returns false when
         Build.VERSION.SDK_INT < Build.VERSION_CODES.O, and both AsyncFunctions
         bail out on that before touching HealthConnectClient. On API 24/25 the
         module reports "unavailable" and the app reads no health data at all,
         which is the documented contract of this availability-gated bridge.

         Scoped to this module's manifest on purpose: this is the module that pulls
         connect-client in, so the override travels with the dependency instead of
         loosening the check for the whole app. -->
    <uses-sdk tools:overrideLibrary="androidx.health.connect.client" />
</manifest>
